     "Syriac is a literary form of Aramaic, the language family associated with the world in which Jesus and His earliest followers lived. It became a major Christian language in the eastern regions of the ancient world.",
     "Christian writers used Syriac to translate Scripture, explain Christian doctrine, compose hymns, and develop liturgical prayers.",
     "As Christianity became established in India, Syriac traditions became deeply influential among the Thomas Christians.",
     "Their church services included readings from Scripture, prayers, hymns, and liturgical celebrations shaped by Eastern Christian practice. Syriac became especially important for the celebration of the Eucharist and other major church services.",
     "One of the historic liturgical traditions associated with the Thomas Christians is the East Syriac tradition. It is also known as the Chaldean or East Syrian tradition in different historical contexts.",
     "The Eucharistic liturgy of Addai and Mari became especially important in this tradition. It is one of the oldest surviving Eucharistic liturgies in Christianity and is associated with the ancient Syriac Christian world.",
     "The presence of this liturgy among Indian Christians demonstrates the close relationship between the Church of India and the Eastern Christian communities.",
     "The use of Syriac did not mean that the people of Kerala abandoned their local language or culture. Malayalam remained the language of daily life, family relationships, and community interaction.",
