    "title": "The Portuguese Encounter: A Challenge to the Ancient Thomas Christian Heritage",
    "scripture": "",
    "narrative": [
     "For centuries, the Thomas Christians of Kerala maintained their relationships with Eastern Christian communities and preserved their own forms of worship and church organisation.",
     "Their history entered a new period when Portuguese explorers arrived on the Indian coast in the late fifteenth century.",
     "In 1498, Vasco da Gama reached Calicut after sailing around the southern tip of Africa. His voyage opened a direct maritime route between Europe and India and began a period of growing Portuguese influence in the region.",
     "The Portuguese were Roman Catholics. Their arrival introduced new forms of religious organisation, European clergy, and connections with the Latin Catholic world.",
     "When they encountered the Thomas Christians, they discovered an established Christian community with ancient traditions and relationships with Eastern churches.",
     "At first, the shared Christian faith created opportunities for cooperation. However, differences soon emerged over church authority, liturgy, theology, and ecclesiastical relationships.",
     "The Thomas Christians had historically used Syriac liturgy and maintained connections with the Eastern Christian world. The Portuguese authorities wanted to bring them more firmly under Roman Catholic and Latin ecclesiastical control.",
     "The Portuguese were also suspicious of the Thomas Christians’ Eastern connections, particularly their relationship with the Persian Church.",
     "These tensions increased over time.",
     "In 1599, Archbishop Aleixo de Menezes organised the Synod of Diamper. The synod attempted to bring the Thomas Christian community into closer conformity with Roman Catholic teaching and Latin church practices.",
     "It introduced changes to church administration, liturgical practices, religious books, and ecclesiastical relationships.",
     "Some of the reforms were accepted, but others caused deep resentment among members of the ancient community.",
     "The Thomas Christians believed that parts of their inherited religious tradition were being altered or restricted. The conflict became a struggle over more than worship practices. It concerned the right of an ancient Indian Christian community to maintain its identity and relationships.",
     "The situation became more serious in the seventeenth century.",
     "In 1653, many Thomas Christians gathered at Mattancherry and took an oath known as the Coonan Cross Oath. They declared their opposition to Portuguese ecclesiastical control.",
     "The oath became one of the most important turning points in the history of the Thomas Christians. It contributed to a major division within the community.",
     "The events that followed were complicated. Some groups sought reconciliation with Rome, while others developed relationships with West Syriac church authorities.",
     "Over time, the community developed different church identities that continue to exist today.",
     "The Portuguese encounter therefore brought both new connections and serious conflict. It introduced the Thomas Christians to wider Catholic networks, but it also disrupted their earlier ecclesiastical relationships and contributed to divisions that shaped Indian Christianity for centuries.",
     "This chapter reminds Christians that unity cannot be built simply by forcing everyone to follow the same cultural or liturgical practices.",
     "True Christian unity requires humility, mutual respect, and a willingness to recognise the legitimate heritage of other communities."
    ],
