     "The growth of the church required organization beyond individual cities. Christian communities existed across a vast territory, and their leaders needed ways to communicate, resolve disagreements, and establish common practices.",
     "A significant development occurred in 410 CE, when a church council was held at Seleucia-Ctesiphon under the leadership of Catholicos Isaac. The council helped organize the church and establish a clearer structure of leadership.",
     "Another important council took place in 424 CE. The bishops declared that the Catholicos-Patriarch should exercise authority within the Persian church without routinely appealing to church leaders in the Roman Empire. This decision reflected both the practical needs of a large eastern church and the political realities of living under a Persian ruler who viewed the Roman Empire as a rival.",
