     "They feared that Christians might sympathize with the Roman emperor or act as supporters of a foreign power.",
     "This suspicion contributed to serious persecution during the reign of Shapur II in the fourth century.",
     "Beginning around 339 CE, Christians in parts of the Persian Empire faced intense pressure. Churches were attacked, Christian leaders were arrested, and many believers were executed. The persecution continued for decades, although its intensity varied across regions and periods.",
     "One of the most remembered figures associated with this persecution was Bishop Simeon bar Sabba’e of Seleucia-Ctesiphon. According to Christian accounts, he refused to abandon his faith or cooperate with demands that threatened the church. He was executed around 341 CE, together with other Christians.",
     "The persecution became a defining part of the Church of the East’s memory. Christian communities preserved accounts of martyrs who remained faithful even when threatened with death.",
