     "One of the most remarkable achievements of the Church of the East was the arrival of Christianity in China during the Tang dynasty.",
     "The Tang dynasty ruled China from 618 to 907 CE. It was a period of significant cultural development, international trade, artistic achievement, and interaction with foreign peoples.",
     "The Chinese capital, Chang’an, was one of the great cities of the world. Merchants, diplomats, scholars, monks, and travelers arrived from many different regions.",
     "Religious traditions from outside China also entered the country. Buddhism had already established a strong presence, while other religious movements were introduced through trade and migration.",
     "It was within this international environment that eastern Christianity arrived.",
     "According to the famous Xi’an Stele, a Christian missionary named Alopen arrived in Chang’an in 635 CE during the reign of Emperor Taizong.",
     "The stele describes the arrival of the Christian teaching and the emperor’s interest in examining its message.",
     "The Chinese imperial court allowed the Christian community to develop, and Christian texts were translated into Chinese. The religion was described using the term Jingjiao, commonly understood as the Luminous Religion.",
     "This translation effort was especially significant. Christianity was being expressed through Chinese language and concepts so that people in China could understand its message.",
     "The Christians who entered China belonged to the eastern Syriac Christian tradition. They brought their Scriptures, prayers, teachings, and forms of worship, but they also engaged with Chinese culture.",
     "They faced the challenge of communicating Christian ideas in a society with its own philosophical and religious vocabulary. Translators had to find ways to explain the nature of God, the incarnation of Christ, sin, salvation, and eternal life.",
     "In 781 CE, a monument known as the Xi’an Stele was erected. Its full title is commonly translated as The Stele Commemorating the Propagation of the Luminous Religion of Daqin in China.",
     "The monument contains Chinese inscriptions and Syriac names. It records the history of Christianity in China and describes the faith’s teachings and relationship with the imperial government.",
     "The stele is one of the most important surviving pieces of evidence for early Christianity in China.",
     "It demonstrates that Christianity had established a recognized presence in China centuries before the arrival of European Catholic missionaries in the sixteenth century.",
     "The Tang Christian community did not remain permanently secure. Political and religious circumstances changed, and in 845 CE Emperor Wuzong ordered a major suppression of foreign religions, including Buddhism and other non-Chinese religious traditions. The Christian community was also affected.",
     "Over time, Christianity’s visibility in China declined, although its influence did not disappear from every part of Asia.",
     "The Xi’an Stele remains a powerful witness to the meeting of Christianity and Chinese civilization. It shows that Christians were already attempting to communicate the gospel through Chinese language, culture, and intellectual traditions more than a thousand years ago.",
     "This history reminds us that the gospel can enter a new civilization without requiring that civilization to abandon every aspect of its cultural identity."
