     "The twentieth century brought enormous political changes to China.",
     "In 1949, the People’s Republic of China was established under the leadership of the Chinese Communist Party. The new government introduced major changes to political institutions, education, land ownership, economic life, and religious organization.",
     "Religious communities were increasingly expected to operate within government-approved structures. Foreign missionary organizations faced restrictions, and many foreign missionaries left China during the early years of the new government.",
     "In 1950, some Chinese Protestant leaders issued the Christian Manifesto, which encouraged Chinese Protestant churches to support the new political environment and pursue a form of church life independent of foreign control.",
     "In 1954, the Three-Self Patriotic Movement was formally established. Its name reflected three principles: self-government, self-support, and self-propagation.",
     "The movement sought to establish Protestant church structures that were not dependent on foreign missionary organizations.",
     "Some Christians supported this approach, believing it provided a way for churches to continue operating in China. Others were concerned about government control and the restrictions placed on church life.",
     "In 1958, many Protestant congregations were reorganized into fewer officially recognized churches. Religious activities increasingly came under government supervision.",
     "A major turning point occurred during the Cultural Revolution, which began in 1966 and lasted until 1976.",
     "During this period, religious institutions faced severe persecution. Churches were closed, Bibles and religious materials were confiscated or destroyed, and public worship was heavily restricted. Many pastors and believers were criticized, imprisoned, sent to labor camps, or otherwise mistreated.",
     "Christian families experienced tremendous pressure. Some believers lost their jobs or were separated from loved ones. Others were forced to remain silent about their faith.",
     "Despite these conditions, Christianity did not disappear.",
     "Some Christians gathered secretly in homes. Others continued praying privately or teaching their children. Believers memorized Scripture when printed Bibles were unavailable and encouraged one another through personal relationships.",
     "Pastors and church leaders who survived the period later helped rebuild Christian communities.",
     "The Cultural Revolution ended after the death of Mao Zedong in 1976 and the political changes that followed. Religious activity gradually became possible again, although it remained subject to government regulation.",
     "The experience of Chinese Protestant Christians during this period demonstrates the importance of faith, community, and perseverance.",
     "It also reminds us of the suffering experienced by people whose religious freedom is restricted. Christians should value the freedom to worship and should defend the dignity and religious rights of all people."
