     "Korean believers met to study Christian teaching, pray, and encourage one another. They shared religious books and introduced friends and relatives to the message of Jesus Christ.",
     "The early Church was largely organized by laypeople. These believers were not ordained priests, but they took responsibility for teaching and supporting their growing community.",
     "One important feature of the early Korean Catholic movement was its emphasis on equality before God. Christian teaching challenged the idea that spiritual importance depended on family background or social position.",
     "Some members came from educated and influential families, while others came from less privileged backgrounds. The Christian message gave them a shared identity as followers of Jesus Christ.",
     "However, the absence of ordained priests created a serious problem. The Catholic Church traditionally depends on priests for the celebration of the Eucharist and the administration of certain sacraments.",
     "The Korean community initially attempted to organize religious activities according to its limited understanding of Catholic practice. When it learned more about the Church’s requirements, it recognized the need for proper sacramental leadership.",
     "Korean Catholics therefore communicated with church authorities in China and requested priests.",
     "In 1794, the Chinese priest James Zhou Wenmo entered Korea secretly. He became the first Catholic priest to serve the growing Korean Catholic community.",
     "His arrival was an important development. Korean believers could now receive the sacraments and participate more fully in the sacramental life of the Catholic Church.",
     "Zhou Wenmo worked among the believers despite the dangers involved. Catholic Christianity was still viewed with suspicion by the Korean government, and foreign religious activity was not welcomed.",
     "The Church continued to grow, attracting people from different backgrounds. Women also played important roles in the development of Catholic communities. They taught children, supported religious gatherings, shared Christian writings, and helped maintain the faith within families.",
     "Korean Catholics were increasingly committed to the belief that Jesus Christ was the center of their spiritual lives. This commitment sometimes brought them into conflict with traditional expectations.",
     "The early community’s growth demonstrated that Christianity had become more than an intellectual interest among scholars. It was developing into a living religious community with worship, instruction, leadership, and shared responsibilities.",
     "The arrival of Father Zhou also revealed the importance of cooperation between local believers and the wider Church. Korean Christians had begun the movement themselves, but they also recognized the need for spiritual guidance and sacramental support.",
     "The early Korean Catholic Church was still small and vulnerable, but its foundations were becoming stronger."
