     "One of the most important figures in this history was Francis Xavier, a Spanish-born Jesuit missionary and one of the founding members of the Society of Jesus.",
     "Xavier had already spent time preaching in India and other parts of Asia. During his missionary work, he encountered a Japanese man commonly identified as Anjirō, also known as Yajiro, who had fled Japan after becoming involved in a serious personal conflict. Anjirō met Xavier in Malacca and introduced him to Japanese language and culture.",
     "Through these conversations, Xavier became interested in bringing Christianity to Japan. He believed the Japanese people were capable of understanding Christian teaching and engaging deeply with questions of faith.",
     "In 1549, Xavier arrived in Kagoshima, in southern Japan, accompanied by Anjirō and other companions. His arrival marked the beginning of the major Jesuit missionary movement in Japan.",
     "Xavier began learning Japanese and preaching about Jesus Christ. He explained Christian beliefs concerning God, creation, sin, salvation, and eternal life. However, communicating Christian theology was not easy. Some Japanese religious concepts did not have direct equivalents in European Christian vocabulary, and Xavier had to find ways to explain unfamiliar teachings.",
     "He also encountered established Buddhist traditions and religious institutions. Japan already possessed sophisticated philosophical, spiritual, and cultural systems. Christianity therefore entered a society with its own deep religious heritage rather than an empty spiritual landscape.",
