    "scripture": "",
    "narrative": [
     "The Armenian church developed its own theological and institutional identity during the early centuries of Christianity. One of the most significant events in its history was the debate surrounding the Council of Chalcedon in AD 451.",
     "The council was convened to address questions about the identity of Jesus Christ. Christians were seeking language that could express their belief that Jesus was fully divine and fully human.",
     "The Council of Chalcedon affirmed that Christ exists in two natures, divine and human, united in one person without confusion, change, division, or separation.",
     "Many churches accepted this formulation. However, other Christian communities, particularly in Egypt, Syria, and Armenia, did not accept the council’s language or the theological interpretation associated with it.",
     "The Armenian church developed a position within the wider non-Chalcedonian tradition. It is commonly described as Oriental Orthodox and uses the theological language of miaphysitism.",
     "This teaching emphasizes the united divine-human nature of the incarnate Christ. It does not mean that Christ’s humanity was destroyed or absorbed into his divinity. Armenian Christians confess Jesus Christ as fully divine and fully human.",
     "The Armenian church did not participate directly in the Council of Chalcedon in the same way as the bishops who attended from the Roman Empire. Armenia was experiencing major political and military difficulties at the time.",
     "In AD 451, Armenian forces fought the Persian army at the Battle of Avarayr. The battle is remembered as a defining event in Armenian Christian history.",
     "The Armenian commander Vardan Mamikonian led Armenian forces in resistance to Persian attempts to suppress Christian religious practices and strengthen Zoroastrian influence.",
     "Although the Armenian forces were defeated militarily, the battle became a powerful symbol of resistance and religious perseverance.",
     "The conflict was part of a broader struggle between the Armenian nobility and the Sasanian Persian Empire. Religious freedom was a major issue, but political independence, aristocratic power, and imperial control were also important factors.",
     "In later decades, Armenian leaders continued to resist attempts to impose religious uniformity. The Treaty of Nvarsak in AD 484 helped secure a greater degree of religious freedom for Armenia under Persian rule.",
     "The Armenian church gradually developed a distinct relationship with the wider Oriental Orthodox family. Its leaders maintained connections with other non-Chalcedonian churches while preserving their own liturgical language, calendar, and ecclesiastical traditions.",
     "The Council of Dvin in the sixth century became important in the Armenian church’s formal rejection of Chalcedonian theology, although the development of its position occurred over time rather than through one single event.",
     "The history of the Armenian church demonstrates how theological conviction, national identity, and political struggle sometimes became closely connected."
    ],
