     "Georgia, located in the Caucasus region between the Black Sea and the Caspian Sea, developed one of the oldest Christian traditions in the world.",
     "In ancient times, eastern Georgia was known as Kartli, while western Georgia included the historical region of Colchis. Greek and Roman writers also used the name Iberia for the eastern Georgian kingdom, which should not be confused with the Iberian Peninsula in Europe.",
     "Before Christianity became established, Georgian religious life included local pagan traditions as well as influences from Greek, Persian, and neighboring religious cultures.",
     "Christianity reached the region during the early centuries of the church. Georgian tradition remembers the missionary work of Saint Nino as the decisive event in the conversion of the kingdom.",
     "According to traditional accounts, Nino was a Christian woman who came from the Christian world of the eastern Roman Empire. She traveled into the kingdom of Kartli and began preaching about Jesus Christ.",
     "Her life is surrounded by religious traditions and accounts of miracles. These stories became important parts of Georgian Christian memory, although individual details cannot all be independently verified.",
     "Nino reportedly gained the attention of Queen Nana, who was suffering from a serious illness. After Nino prayed for her, the queen recovered and became a Christian.",
     "King Mirian III initially resisted the new faith. However, according to Georgian tradition, he experienced a crisis during a hunting expedition and called upon the Christian God for help.",
     "After his experience, the king accepted Christianity and supported its establishment throughout the kingdom.",
     "Around the 330s, Christianity became the official religion of Kartli. The precise chronology is debated, but the fourth-century conversion is firmly embedded in Georgian history.",
     "The conversion of the royal family helped establish Christian institutions. Churches were built, clergy were trained, and the new faith gradually spread throughout the population.",
     "Early Georgian Christianity developed close relationships with neighboring Christian communities, particularly the churches of Antioch and Constantinople. At the same time, it retained a distinctive cultural and linguistic identity.",
     "The first major Christian centers became important places of worship and education. Mtskheta, the ancient capital of Kartli, became especially significant in Georgian religious history.",
     "The Svetitskhoveli Cathedral, located in Mtskheta, is associated with important Georgian Christian traditions and became one of the nation’s most revered religious sites.",
     "Saint Nino became one of the most beloved figures in Georgian history. Her cross, traditionally described as being made from grapevine branches bound with her own hair, became an important symbol of Georgian Christianity.",
     "Georgia’s conversion demonstrated the influence that individual missionaries and local believers could have on an entire kingdom.",
