     "Uchimura became a Christian during his youth and later developed an approach to Christianity that emphasized personal faith, biblical authority, and independence from foreign ecclesiastical institutions.",
     "He became known for his concept of Mukyōkai, often translated as the Nonchurch Movement.",
     "Uchimura believed that Christian faith should not depend entirely on formal church structures. He emphasized the importance of Scripture, personal devotion, and direct commitment to Jesus Christ.",
     "His approach was controversial among some Japanese Christians, but it influenced generations of believers who sought to develop an indigenous expression of Christian faith.",
     "Uchimura also became known for his criticism of excessive nationalism and his concern for ethical responsibility.",
     "Another influential Japanese Christian was Toyohiko Kagawa, who lived from 1888 to 1960.",
     "Kagawa became a Christian as a young man and devoted much of his life to serving poor and marginalized communities.",
     "He worked among people living in poverty in Kobe and became involved in labor reform, cooperative movements, and social welfare.",
     "Kagawa believed that Christian faith should lead to practical action on behalf of people suffering from poverty and injustice.",
