     "Indonesian Christians have contributed to education, healthcare, government, music, literature, and national development.",
     "Indonesia’s national philosophy, Pancasila, provides a framework for national identity and recognizes belief in God as one of its foundational principles. The country officially recognizes several religions, including Protestantism and Catholicism.",
     "However, religious relations vary considerably across the archipelago. Some Christian communities experience peaceful coexistence, while others have encountered restrictions, social tensions, or violence.",
     "The communal conflicts in Maluku and parts of Central Sulawesi around the turn of the twenty-first century involved both Christian and Muslim communities. These conflicts were shaped by political competition, migration, local grievances, and religious identity. They should not be reduced to religious differences alone.",
     "In Malaysia, Christians form a minority in a society where Islam is the official religion of the federation.",
     "Christianity has a particularly significant presence in Sabah and Sarawak, where many indigenous communities are Christian. Other Christian communities include Chinese, Indian, Eurasian, and other Malaysian populations.",
     "The churches of Sabah and Sarawak include Catholic, Anglican, Methodist, Baptist, and other Protestant traditions.",
