     "China, Japan, and South Korea demonstrate three different experiences of Christianity within East Asian societies.",
     "In China, Christianity includes Catholic, Protestant, and independent Christian communities.",
     "The history of Christianity in China reaches back to the Tang dynasty, when Church of the East missionaries introduced the faith in the seventh century.",
     "Catholic missions developed during the Yuan dynasty and expanded again during the late Ming and Qing periods. Protestant missions began in the nineteenth century, although Chinese believers and local church leaders became increasingly important in developing the churches.",
     "The establishment of the People’s Republic of China in 1949 brought significant changes. Christian institutions were reorganized, foreign missionary organizations were removed, and religious activity became subject to state regulation.",
     "During the Cultural Revolution from 1966 to 1976, religious institutions experienced severe disruption and persecution.",
     "Following the late 1970s, churches gradually reopened and Christian communities expanded in many regions.",
     "Today, Chinese Christianity includes registered churches, independent house churches, Catholic communities, and other networks. The experiences of Christians differ according to location, church affiliation, and government policy.",
     "Japan has a different Christian history.",
     "Catholic Christianity first developed during the sixteenth century through the work of Francis Xavier and other missionaries. Christianity experienced periods of rapid growth and severe persecution.",
     "Following the lifting of restrictions in the nineteenth century, Catholic, Protestant, and Orthodox communities developed again.",
     "However, Christians remain a small minority in modern Japan. Japanese churches contribute to education, social welfare, literature, humanitarian work, and interreligious dialogue.",
