     "During the seventeenth century, another European power entered Sri Lankan history. The Dutch East India Company sought to control the island’s valuable trade, particularly its cinnamon production and strategic coastal ports. The Dutch gradually challenged Portuguese authority and captured important territories.",
     "The Dutch took control of Colombo in 1656 and completed the capture of the main Portuguese coastal possessions by 1658. Their rule introduced a new political and religious environment. Unlike the Portuguese, who promoted Roman Catholicism, the Dutch authorities supported the Reformed Protestant tradition associated with the Dutch Reformed Church.",
     "Dutch Reformed Christianity became the favored church in territories under Dutch control. The colonial administration supported church buildings, clergy, schools, and religious instruction. Dutch ministers and teachers worked to establish Protestant Christianity among local communities.",
     "The Dutch authorities viewed the spread of the Reformed faith as part of their religious and political responsibility. They established churches and encouraged local people to attend Protestant worship. In some areas, Catholic institutions were restricted, and Catholic clergy faced difficulties because the Dutch administration regarded Roman Catholicism with suspicion.",
     "The Dutch also developed a system of Christian education. Schools were established in different districts, and children were taught reading, writing, religious instruction, and basic subjects. Education helped strengthen the influence of the Reformed Church and created new opportunities for local people to enter colonial administrative and professional life.",
     "However, the relationship between the Dutch Reformed Church and local society was complicated. Some people accepted Protestant Christianity for religious reasons, while others participated because church membership could bring educational, economic, or social advantages. The colonial system sometimes encouraged outward conformity without necessarily producing deep personal conviction.",
     "The Dutch authorities also made use of local languages in religious instruction. Sinhala and Tamil were important for communicating Christian teaching to the population. The translation of religious materials and the training of local teachers helped extend Protestant influence beyond the Dutch-speaking community.",
