     "Central Asia was essential to this expansion. Cities such as Merv, Samarkand, and other settlements served as centres of commerce and cultural exchange. Merchants carried goods across the region, while religious communities established relationships with people from different backgrounds. Christian inscriptions, manuscripts, and archaeological discoveries provide evidence of this wider presence.",
     "The Tibetan plateau was connected to these networks through several routes. Western Tibetan regions had links with Ladakh and Kashmir. Other routes connected Central Asia with the areas around the Tarim Basin and western China. These routes were not simple roads but changing networks of mountain passes, valleys, trading settlements, and caravan paths.",
     "A Christian traveller could therefore approach the Tibetan world from several directions. Yet difficult geography made permanent settlement challenging. High altitude, severe weather, long distances, and political boundaries created obstacles for people who wished to travel or establish religious institutions.",
