     "The Church of the East’s presence in China offers a useful comparison. The famous Xi’an Stele, erected in 781, provides evidence of Christianity in Tang China and describes the arrival and development of the Christian faith there. This does not prove an equivalent Christian establishment in Tibet, but it demonstrates how far eastern Christian networks could reach.",
     "The story of the Church of the East and Tibetan trade routes reminds us that the Christian history of Asia was interconnected. Regions that today are studied separately were often linked through commerce, diplomacy, language, and religious travel. Tibet was part of this larger world of movement, even though the evidence for an organized Christian church there remains limited."
