     "People who became Christians sometimes faced opposition from their families and local communities. Religious identity was closely connected with family traditions, festivals, social relationships, and inherited customs. Changing one’s religious affiliation could therefore be interpreted as rejecting more than a set of beliefs; it could be seen as leaving behind a family and community heritage.",
     "Legal restrictions also created difficulties. During the monarchy, conversion and missionary activity could carry serious consequences. Christian believers sometimes faced arrest, questioning, or imprisonment, particularly when authorities believed that religious activity violated the law.",
     "The Christian community nevertheless continued to grow. Small congregations met in homes, and believers developed networks of prayer and fellowship. Some Christians travelled to India for education or religious training and later returned to Nepal to serve their communities.",
     "The monarchy period therefore shaped the character of Nepali Christianity. Because foreign missionary institutions were restricted, local believers became increasingly important in maintaining churches and sharing their faith. The development of Nepali Christian communities depended on personal relationships, local leadership, and networks that could function under difficult circumstances.",
     "In 1990, Nepal experienced a major political transformation that introduced a constitutional monarchy and expanded political freedoms. This created a changing environment for religious communities, although restrictions on conversion remained an important issue. The monarchy eventually ended in 2008, when Nepal became a federal democratic republic.",
